PRIVATE REFLECTION: My current leaning remains **A. overestimate**, under the standard epidemiologic meaning of “incidence rate ratio” as cases per person-time over follow-up. Let \(p\) be the completely protected fraction for an all-or-nothing vaccine; this \(p\) is the true average per-exposure vaccine efficacy. If unvaccinated hazard is \(\lambda\), the simple mixture hazard among vaccinees would be \((1-p)\lambda\), giving \(\mathrm{IRR}=1-p\) and \(1-\mathrm{IRR}=p\). But a person-time IRR includes protected vaccinees, who never become infected and keep contributing infection-free person-time, while unprotected vaccinees can be infected and removed from the denominator. This dilutes the vaccinated rate below \((1-p)\lambda\). Formally,
\[
\mathrm{IRR}
=
\frac{(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
{p\lambda T+(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
<1-p,
\]
so \(1-\mathrm{IRR}>p\). Thus it overestimates per-exposure vaccine efficacy.

The C camp (colleagues 7 and 10) is correct only if “incidence rate ratio” means a per-exposure risk ratio, an initial/instantaneous hazard ratio, a cumulative risk ratio, or if protected vaccinees are excluded from the denominator or follow-up is very short/rare infection. That is the key unresolved estimand ambiguity. In round 2, the majority (1–6, 8, 9) favors A; 7 and 10 favor C. I would keep A for the usual person-time IRR, while noting C is defensible if the question explicitly intends a pure per-exposure parameter. No one has argued for underestimate.

